In response to heightened security concerns, the xFusionCorp Industries security team has opted for custom Apache users for their web applications. 
Each user is tailored specifically for an application, enhancing security measures. 
Your task is to create a custom Apache user according to the outlined specifications:

a. Create a user named mark on App server 3 within the Stratos Datacenter.

b. Assign a unique UID 1517 and designate the home directory as /var/www/mark.

SOLUTION
Steps to Create the Custom Apache User
First, SSH into App server 3:

bash
ssh username@app-server-3
# Or use the specific hostname/IP provided in your environment
Switch to root or use sudo:

bash
sudo su -
# Or prefix all commands with sudo
Create the user mark with UID 1517 and home directory /var/www/mark:

bash
useradd -u 1517 -d /var/www/mark -m mark
Explanation of flags:

-u 1517 → Sets the unique UID to 1517

-d /var/www/mark → Sets the home directory to /var/www/mark

-m → Creates the home directory if it doesn't exist

mark → The username

Verify the user was created correctly:

bash
id mark
Expected output:

text
uid=1517(mark) gid=1517(mark) groups=1517(mark)
Verify the home directory:

bash
ls -ld /var/www/mark
Expected output:

text
drwx------ 2 mark mark 4096 ... /var/www/mark
If needed for Apache to serve content, set appropriate permissions:

bash
chmod 755 /var/www/mark
chown mark:mark /var/www/mark
Optional: Configure Apache to Run as This User
If you want Apache to actually run as this user, you would need to edit the Apache configuration:

bash
# Edit the Apache config file
vi /etc/httpd/conf/httpd.conf   # On RHEL/CentOS
# or
vi /etc/apache2/apache2.conf    # On Debian/Ubuntu
Change:

text
User apache
Group apache
To:

text
User mark
Group mark
Then restart Apache:

bash
systemctl restart httpd    # RHEL/CentOS
# or
systemctl restart apache2  # Debian/Ubuntu
